---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import RateChart from "../../components/RateChart.astro";
import BillChart from "../../components/BillChart.astro";
import CostChart from "../../components/CostChart.astro";
import GapChart from "../../components/GapChart.astro";
---
<Base title="Electricity prices and costs" description="How Virginia home electricity prices and Dominion's typical bill have changed, and what it costs to make power from solar, wind, gas, nuclear, coal and batteries.">
  <div class="band">
    <h1>Electricity prices and what power costs to make</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      What Virginia homes pay for electricity, how that has changed, and what different power sources cost to build and run.
      Every number links to its source, and each dataset can be downloaded.
    </p>
    <nav class="jump" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#rates-title">Home prices since 1990</a>
      <a href="#bill-title">Dominion's typical bill</a>
      <a href="#gap-title">Prices vs. the cost of making power</a>
      <a href="#costs-title">Cost by power source</a>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <div class="band stack">
    <RateChart />
    <BillChart />
    <GapChart />
    <CostChart />
  </div>

  <div class="band narrow prose">
    <h2>How to read these together</h2>
    <p>
      Your bill pays for much more than making electricity. It also covers power lines, poles and substations, programs the
      law requires, and the utility's profit, so a cheap power source does not translate one-for-one into a cheaper bill.
      The cost chart compares sources on the same footing: the average cost per megawatt-hour of power over a plant's life.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cost of making power from a new plant also depends on where and how it is built, on interest rates, and, for gas and
      coal, on fuel prices, which can swing from year to year. Plants that are already paid off cost much less to keep running,
      which is why the chart shows them separately.
    </p>
    <p>
      Utility rate cases at the State Corporation Commission decide which of these costs end up on your bill. See the{" "}
      <a href="/cases/">cases we track</a> and their <a href="/calendar/">comment deadlines</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .jump { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px 20px; margin-top: 16px; font-size: 15px; }
  .stack { display: grid; gap: clamp(48px, 7vw, 80px); padding-top: 0; }
</style>
